(   [Problem Statement]: Evaluate the exponential indeterminate limits: \(i\) lim_{x -> 0} \(sin x / x\)^\(1 / x^2\) [Form 1^infinity], \(ii\) lim_{x -> 0+} x^\(sin x\) [Form 0^0].) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Part \(i\): Evaluate L = lim_{x -> 0} \(sin x / x\)^\(1 / x^2\):) Tj
T*
(   - Since lim_{x -> 0} \(sin x / x\) = 1 and lim 1/x^2 = infinity, this is of indeterminate form) Tj
T*
(   1^infinity.) Tj
T*
(   - Take natural logarithm:) Tj
T*
(   ln L = lim_{x -> 0} ln [ \(sin x / x\)^\(1 / x^2\) ] = lim_{x -> 0} [ ln\(sin x / x\) / x^2 ] \(Form) Tj
T*
(   0/0\).) Tj
T*
(   - Use Taylor series expansion of sin x / x:) Tj
T*
(   sin x / x = [ x - x^3/6 + x^5/120 - ... ] / x = 1 - x^2/6 + x^4/120 - ...) Tj
T*
(   - Now use ln\(1 + u\) = u - u^2/2 + ... where u = -x^2/6 + x^4/120:) Tj
T*
(   ln\(sin x / x\) = \(-x^2/6 + x^4/120\) - \(1/2\)*\(-x^2/6\)^2 + ... = -x^2/6 + O\(x^4\).) Tj
T*
(   - Substitute into limit:) Tj
T*
(   ln L = lim_{x -> 0} [ \( -x^2/6 + O\(x^4\) \) / x^2 ] = lim_{x -> 0} [ -1/6 + O\(x^2\) ] = -1/6.) Tj
T*
(   - Exponentiate to obtain L:) Tj
T*
(   L = e^\(ln L\) = e^\(-1/6\) = 1 / e^\(1/6\).) Tj
T*
(   2. Part \(ii\): Evaluate L = lim_{x -> 0+} x^\(sin x\):) Tj
T*
(   - At x = 0+: base -> 0, exponent -> 0 \(Form 0^0\).) Tj
T*
(   - Take natural logarithm:) Tj
T*
(   ln L = lim_{x -> 0+} ln\(x^\(sin x\)\) = lim_{x -> 0+} [ sin x * ln x ] \(Form 0 * \(-infinity\)\).) Tj
T*
(   - Convert to 0/0 or infinity/infinity form:) Tj
T*
ET

